4️⃣ Polymorphism — "Same Name, Different Behavior" 🎪

The idea: "Poly" = many, "morph" = forms. The SAME method name behaves DIFFERENTLY depending on the object calling it. Two types:

Compile-time (Method Overloading) — same method name, different parameters
Runtime (Method Overriding) — child class provides its OWN version of a parent's method

Real analogy: Press "brake" 🛑 — on a bicycle, you squeeze a lever. On a car, you press a pedal. On a plane, you deploy reverse thrusters. Same ACTION ("brake"), completely different execution depending on the vehicle. That's polymorphism.

java
// Parent class
class Animal {
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Some generic animal sound... 🐾");
    }
}

// Child classes OVERRIDE makeSound() differently
class Dog extends Animal {
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Woof Woof! 🐶");
    }
}

class Cat extends Animal {
    @Override
    void makeSound() {
        System.out.println("Meow! 🐱");
    }
}

// THE MAGIC 🪄 — Runtime Polymorphism
Animal myPet = new Dog();
myPet.makeSound(); // prints "Woof Woof!" — even though the variable type is "Animal"!

myPet = new Cat();
myPet.makeSound(); // now prints "Meow!" — SAME line of code, different behavior!

Look at that last part carefully 👀 — myPet.makeSound() is the EXACT same line of code, but it behaves differently depending on WHAT object myPet actually is at runtime. That's the magic of polymorphism — Java figures out at runtime which version to call. 🔥

Bonus — Method Overloading (compile-time polymorphism), quick example:

java
class Calculator {
    int add(int a, int b) {
        return a + b;
    }
    double add(double a, double b) { // same name "add", different params
        return a + b;
    }
}

Same method name add, but Java decides WHICH one to run based on the arguments you pass. That's overloading.

🎤 One-liner: "Polymorphism allows the same method to behave differently based on the object calling it — achieved through method overriding (runtime) or overloading (compile-time)."